    <div class="callout callout-info">
      <strong>🎯 Step 2 of 4:</strong> Game design is not about coding or drawing; it is about creating rules, choices, and goals that make playing fun.
    </div>

    <h2>What is Game Design?</h2>
    <p>
      Game design is the architecture of play. While a programmer writes code and an artist creates visual assets, the game designer crafts the systems that make those elements work together. Game design determines how fast a character moves, how high they jump, what obstacles stand in their way, and how the game rewards success.
    </p>

    <h2>The MDA Framework</h2>
    <p>
      The Mechanics, Dynamics, and Aesthetics (MDA) framework helps developers understand how games function from both the developer's side and the player's side:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Mechanics:</strong> The base rules, algorithms, and code set by the developer. This includes character jump height, weapon damage values, and enemy health points.</li>
      <li><strong>Dynamics:</strong> How those mechanics behave when a real player starts interacting with them. For instance, combining high jump mechanics with moving platforms creates dynamic parkour gameplay.</li>
      <li><strong>Aesthetics:</strong> The emotional response the player feels while playing. This can include feelings of discovery, suspense, mastery, competition, or mystery.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Developers build <em>Mechanics</em>, which combine during play into system <em>Dynamics</em>, which ultimately yield emotional <em>Aesthetics</em> for the player.
    </p>

    <h2>Core Design Pillars</h2>
    <p>
      Design pillars are three or four core guidelines that define what makes your project unique. Every feature, weapon, artwork, or music track you consider adding must support at least one of these pillars.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, if one of your design pillars is <em>"Fast, Non-Stop Movement,"</em> adding a slow inventory management menu breaks that pillar. Design pillars give you a clear compass whenever you are unsure about adding a feature.
    </p>

