    <h2>The Core Loop</h2>
    <p>
      The core loop is the basic cycle of actions that players repeat throughout the entire game. A solid core loop keeps players coming back for more.
    </p>
    <p>A standard core loop follows four basic steps:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Action:</strong> The player performs a primary action, such as jumping across platform gaps, shooting enemy targets, or harvesting wood.</li>
      <li><strong>Reward:</strong> The game instantly rewards the player with gold coins, experience points, or loot drops.</li>
      <li><strong>Upgrade:</strong> The player spends earned rewards to buy better gear, unlock stronger character abilities, or open new map zones.</li>
      <li><strong>Challenge:</strong> The player uses their upgraded powers to take on tougher challenges, restarting the core cycle.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Scope and Preventing Scope Creep</h2>
    <p>
      Scope represents the total amount of work, art assets, code features, and level designs required to complete a game. Scope creep happens when you keep adding new features—like mini-games, extra playable characters, or massive open maps—while building the game. Scope creep causes projects to take years longer than planned, leading to burnout.
    </p>
    <p>To manage scope and keep your game achievable:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Build a Minimum Viable Product (MVP):</strong> Strip your idea down to the absolute smallest playable version that tests whether the core mechanics are fun.</li>
      <li><strong>Cut Unnecessary Features:</strong> If a secondary feature takes three weeks to program but adds only a tiny bit of fun, drop it.</li>
      <li><strong>Cut Scope Early:</strong> When behind schedule, cut extra level sections or extra weapons until only the essential core loop remains.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout callout-warning">
      <strong>⚠️ Prototyping Rule:</strong> If your first game concept has "procedurally generated infinite universe with multiplayer MMO economies," pump the brakes immediately. Start with a self-contained 2D mechanic you can polish in two weekends.
    </div>

